Nested Interface

Interview Questions

1. What is a Nested Interface?
-->A Nested Interface is an interface declared inside a class or another interface.

It is used to group related interfaces together.

Example:

class Vehicle {

    interface Engine {
        void start();
    }
}




2. Why do we declare an interface inside a class?
-->We declare an interface inside a class to:

      Organize related code.
      Improve code readability.
      Hide the interface from unrelated classes.
      Represent functionality closely related to the outer class.





3. How do you implement a nested interface?
-->Use the outer class name followed by the interface name.
      Syntax:
      
            class Outer {
                interface Inner {
                    void display();
                }
            }
            
            class Demo implements Outer.Inner {
            
                public void display() {
                    System.out.println("Nested Interface");
                }
            }






4. What are the access rules for nested interfaces?
-->A nested interface can be declared inside a class or another interface.
   It can have access modifiers like public, protected, private, or default (when inside a class).
   All methods in an interface are public abstract by default.
   All variables are public static final by default.
   
   To implement it outside the outer class, use:
   
   Outer.InterfaceName






5. What is a real-time use case?
-->A common real-time example is Java Event Handling.

For example:
         MouseListener
         ActionListener
         KeyListener

Nested interfaces are also used in frameworks and APIs to keep related interfaces together.






Programs

1. Write a Student Management System using a member inner class.

   Outer class: School (name, location)

   Inner class: Student (name, rollNo, grade)

   Display student info along with school info.
-->class School {

    String schoolName = "ABC School";
    String location = "Hyderabad";

    class Student {

        String name;
        int rollNo;
        String grade;

        Student(String name, int rollNo, String grade) {
            this.name = name;
            this.rollNo = rollNo;
            this.grade = grade;
        }

        void display() {
            System.out.println("School Name : " + schoolName);
            System.out.println("Location    : " + location);
            System.out.println("Student     : " + name);
            System.out.println("Roll No     : " + rollNo);
            System.out.println("Grade       : " + grade);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        School school = new School();
        School.Student student = school.new Student("Rahul", 101, "A");

        student.display();
    }
}







2. Implement a Company class with a static nested class Employee.

   Outer class: Company (companyName)

   Nested class: Employee (id, name, salary)

   Print employee info along with company name.
-->class Company {

    static String companyName = "OpenAI";

    static class Employee {

        int id;
        String name;
        double salary;

        Employee(int id, String name, double salary) {
            this.id = id;
            this.name = name;
            this.salary = salary;
        }

        void display() {
            System.out.println("Company : " + companyName);
            System.out.println("ID      : " + id);
            System.out.println("Name    : " + name);
            System.out.println("Salary  : " + salary);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Company.Employee emp = new Company.Employee(101, "Karthik", 50000);

        emp.display();
    }
}









3. Create a Runnable thread using an anonymous inner class.

   Print numbers 1 to 5 with the thread name.

-->class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Thread t = new Thread(new Runnable() {

            public void run() {

                for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
                    System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName() + " : " + i);
                }
            }
        });

        t.start();
    }
}








4. Write a Calculator interface with the method:
   
   int multiply(int a, int b);

   Provide the implementation using an anonymous inner class.
-->interface Calculator {

